@startuml
!include style.puml
title An action call, and why it reads like a local call

participant "App" as app $usercode
participant "WorkerHaContext" as ctx $worker
participant "PendingRequests" as pc $worker
participant "Broker" as b $broker
participant "Home Assistant" as ha $ha

app -> ctx : callAction('light.turn_on', [...])
ctx -> pc : nextId() → "0:17"
ctx -> pc : expect("0:17") → Future
ctx -> b : **ActionCallRequest** { correlationId: "0:17" }

ctx -> ctx : future->await(TimeoutCancellation)
note right of ctx
  The app's **fiber suspends here**. The worker
  process stays free to handle other events —
  that suspension is the entire "reads like a
  local call" property.
end note

b -> b : record correlationId → worker
b -> ha : call_service { id: 42 }
ha --> b : result { id: 42, success: true }

b -> b : correlate id 42 → "0:17"
b -> ctx : **ActionCallResult** { "0:17", ok }
ctx -> pc : resolve("0:17")
ctx --> app : EventContext

note over b, ha
  The broker must answer **every** request,
  including on failure or a dropped connection.
  A missing reply is not a lost message — it is
  a permanently suspended fiber with nothing in
  the logs. The worker-side timeout is the
  backstop for exactly that.
end note
@enduml
